course: dictionary
lesson: 14426
title: Overstating things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To overstate something is to make it sound bigger, better or more important than it really is. Another word for it is exaggerate. Read the words around it to spot it: «Ella said she had read a thousand books, but she had only read ten.» The word but is the clue. It tells you the real number is much smaller than what Ella said, so she overstated it.
explain: Writers who overstate lose trust, because readers start to wonder what else is untrue. Watch for big words like «always», «never» and «the best ever». They can be signs that something is being overstated. Ask yourself: does this sound bigger than the facts?
explain: The phrase «It's hard to overstate…» does not mean you are making something sound bigger. It means something is extremely important. «It is hard to overstate how useful a map is on a long walk» means a map is really, really useful.
fact: To overstate means to make something sound bigger, better or more important than it is.
fact: Overstate is a verb: overstate, overstates, overstated, overstating.
fact: Another word with the same meaning is exaggerate.
fact: «Overstated» is the past tense: «Sam overstated how fast he ran.»
fact: A good check is to ask: does this sound bigger than the real facts?
exercise: Ella said she had read a thousand books, but she had only read ten. Did Ella overstate? | Yes, she made it sound smaller | No, she never read at all | *Yes, she made it sound more than it was | No, she told the exact truth
exercise: What does overstate mean? | *To make something sound bigger, better or more important than it is | To make something sound quieter and smaller | To say something exactly as it happened | To forget a word
exercise: Sam overstated how fast he ran. Which word means the same as overstate? | measure | whisper | forget | *exaggerate
test: «The advert said the shoes were the best shoes ever made. It overstated the truth.» What does overstated mean here? | *Made it sound better than it really is | Made it sound exactly right | Made it sound worse than it really is | Made it sound very quiet
test: «It is hard to overstate how important water is.» What does this phrase mean? | Water is not important at all | Water is hard to find | *Water is extremely important | Water tastes strange
exam: Max said his drawing took a whole year, but it took one afternoon. What did Max do? | He told the exact time | *He overstated how long it took | He made it sound shorter than it was | He forgot how long it took
